WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.240
Let’s talk about the International Criminal Court

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.480
and why the US is going after it.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.280
The ICC is investigating whether war crimes

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:10.920
were committed in Afghanistan.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.040
Not just by the Taliban and Afghan forces

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.920
but by the US military too.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.760
US President Donald Trump responded by

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.560
imposing sanctions on ICC prosecutors.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.280
So why is the US lashing out at the ICC?

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.040
What happened in Afghanistan?

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.240
And what, if anything, has Israel got to do

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.440
with any of this?

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.000
Let’s get our heads around

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.480
this business of international justice.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.120
The ICC is there to hold the most powerful people

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.720
accountable for the most serious of crimes.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.400
It’s about providing millions of victims with access to justice

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.400
if the country in question is unable

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.520
or unwilling to seek it for them.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.560
That’s why it’s considered the court of last resort.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.760
It’s people like the Rohingya Muslims.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:08.680
They were forced to flee

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.120
a military crackdown in Myanmar.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.080
There’s also the Yazidis in northern Iraq

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.160
brutalised and enslaved by Islamic State fighters.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.960
And the people of Darfur in Sudan who say they were

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.680
subjected to a campaign of terror

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.680
by a government-backed militia.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.000
The ICC was created in 1998 by a treaty called

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.400
the Rome Statute.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.840
Most countries in the world have signed on to it.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.640
And the court works like most courts.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.520
The prosecutor builds a case and takes it to trial.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.800
And the accused has the right to appeal.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.640
Now, a person can be investigated if they’re accused

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.600
of committing crimes in any of the 123 member countries.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.320
Afghanistan is one of them.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.160
Prosecutors can also investigate people

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.920
from countries that aren’t signed up to the ICC.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.280
Like the US.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.000
That’s why Americans are being investigated

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.240
for what happened in Afghanistan.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:59.240
The UN Security Council can also refer cases to the court

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:00.720
like they did with Darfur

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.240
even though Sudan isn’t a member.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.800
Among other places the ICC is now looking into

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.880
atrocities in Afghanistan.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.920
The case is focused on events after 2003

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.120
and alleged crimes by all sides.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.800
Let’s start with the Taliban.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.640
In 2001 the Taliban was in power.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.200
They were seen as an oppressive regime

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.560
that refused to hand over Osama bin Laden.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.240
After the September 11 attacks the US

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.560
went into Afghanistan to push them out.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.640
By 2003 a new Afghan government was in place

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.120
and the US and NATO had control of the military.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.680
But Taliban fighters didn’t go away.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.600
They and other militant groups went on

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.120
to wage a guerilla war.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.400
Not only targeting Afghan forces and their NATO allies.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.000
But civilians too.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.920
And the violence hasn’t stopped.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:17.000
There are attacks nearly every day

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.880
in many parts of Afghanistan.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.480
The ICC is also looking at Afghan forces

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.480
and whether they commited war crimes.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.560
On the list of accusations

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.320
torture and sexual abuse of detainees.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.840
The court has its sights on people

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:36.880
like General Abdul Rashid Dostum

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.800
who until recently was Afghanistan’s vice president.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.480
During the war he led a pro-government militia

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.080
whose fighters are accused of atrocities like killing

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.560
and burying thousands of Taliban prisoners

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.920
in mass graves.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:52.360
At the time his militia was backed by US Special Forces.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:55.400
That’s them riding with General Dostum’s men.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.320
Which brings us to the United States

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:58.880
and what it stands accused of.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.360
For nearly 13 years Americans were the major

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.040
foreign power in Afghanistan

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.400
leading an international coalition that at one point

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.560
in 2010 reached 150,000 foreign soldiers.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:13.960
US Special Forces were mainly there

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.000
to support the Afghan government.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.280
But they’re accused of crimes including the abuse of

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:20.400
prisoners and covering up killings.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.560
The CIA is also being investigated

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:55.320
for its use of torture.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.880
So the prosecutor’s office at the ICC

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.000
gathered all of this evidence on Afghanistan

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.040
and finally got the green light in March to go ahead.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.320
And then President Donald Trump imposed sanctions.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.000
Something the US usually reserves for rogue nations 

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.760
and human rights abusers.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:24.760
They include travel bans and asset freezes

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.600
against the office of the prosecutor.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.840
But resisting the ICC’s jurisdiction

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.080
seems to have been US policy from the beginning.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.880
The Clinton administration only reluctantly

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:44.520
signed the Rome Statute.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.120
And two years later President George W Bush

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:48.440
withdrew from it.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.160
The US then took more steps to protect itself.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:02.080
In 2002 during the Bush era they passed a law

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.360
that allows the US to ignore the ICC.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:07.960
it even authorises the use of military force

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:12.160
to free US nationals detained by or on behalf of the court.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.960
And yet the US has been known to co-operate

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:15.960
with the ICC.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:32.280
But there’s another factor at play here.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:33.480
And that’s Israel.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.360
It’s one of America’s closest allies.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:36.600
And is also being investigated

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:38.920
for suspected crimes in Palestine.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.560
Like the US, Israel isn’t an ICC member.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.000
But Palestine is.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:09.240
In fact earlier this year

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.360
Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:13.440
called for sanctions against the court.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:14.480
And he got what he wanted.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.960
The only way that countries can avoid ICC prosecution

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.960
is if they’re investigating themselves.

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:38.640
That’s what the Afghan government says it’s doing.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:39.840
But many in Afghanistan

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.200
don’t trust their own justice system.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:53.440
The US says it’s already investigated its military.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:55.800
That happened under President Barack Obama.

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:11.480
But the court says those investigations

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:12.800
haven’t gone far enough.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:16.000
International justice is a tricky business.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.560
The court’s been accused of targeting

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:19.160
mostly African suspects.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:22.120
Countries are pulling out to avoid being investigated.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:25.280
And the ICC has no way of enforcing its decisions.

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.040
It relies on countries to co-operate

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:28.760
and hand people over.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:30.720
And then there’s its record.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:33.720
In 18 years it’s convicted four people.

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:35.960
But these are powerful people.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:40.760
Like the Congolese warlord Thomas Lubanga.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:42.160
He was convicted of war crimes

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:44.240
for recruiting child soldiers.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:48.120
And the Bosnian Serb military commander Ratko Mladic.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:51.080
For the Srebrenica genocide and other crimes.

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:54.040
For all its successes and perceived failings

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:58.480
the ICC is still seen by many as important and necessary.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:00.600
Just ask people in Afghanistan.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:30.560
So are those US sanctions going to work?

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:34.200
Well the ICC says it will carry on doing what it’s doing.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:36.160
Which is sort of its job

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.360
to act independently and resist the most powerful

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.720
no matter who they are.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:45.600
